  "summary": "Outline formed by a set of rectangular buildings.",
  "statement": "`buildings[i] = [left, right, height]` (sorted by `left`). Return the skyline's key points `[x, h]`, sorted by\n`x`: each is where the outline's height changes to `h`. The last point has height 0. Consecutive points never\nhave the same height.",
  "constraints": [
    "1 <= buildings.length <= 10^4",
    "0 <= left < right <= 2^31 - 1",
    "1 <= height <= 2^31 - 1"
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "buildings = [[2, 9, 10], [3, 7, 15], [5, 12, 12], [15, 20, 10], [19, 24, 8]]",
      "output": "[[2, 10], [3, 15], [7, 12], [12, 0], [15, 10], [20, 8], [24, 0]]"
    },
    {
      "input": "buildings = [[0, 2, 3], [2, 5, 3]]",
      "output": "[[0, 3], [5, 0]]"
    },
    {
      "input": "buildings = [[1, 2, 1]]",
      "output": "[[1, 1], [2, 0]]"
    }
  ],
  "signature": {
    "method": "getSkyline",
    "params": [
      {
        "name": "buildings",
        "type": "int[][]"
      }
    ],
    "returns": "int[][]"
  },
  "starter_code": "class Solution {\n    public int[][] getSkyline(int[][] buildings) {\n        // TODO: write your solution here\n        return new int[0][];\n    }\n}\n",
  "hints": [
    "The outline can only change at a building's left or right edge.",
    "Sweep over edge x-coordinates in order, keeping the heights of buildings covering x in a max-heap (removing ones that have ended).",
    "for x in sorted edge positions:\n    add buildings starting at x to the heap; drop heap tops whose right <= x\n    h = top height or 0\n    if h != last height: output [x, h]",
    "O(n log n). Lazy removal (dropping ended buildings only when they reach the top) keeps the heap simple."
  ],
  "expected_complexity": {
    "time": "O(n log n)",
    "space": "O(n)"
  },
